## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes real-world SRE orchestration for multi-tenant systems under burst traffic, requiring dynamic budgets, fairness, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Fusion of signals: latency, error rate, queue depth
- Admission control and load shedding
- Safe rollback plan with tenant-level validation
- Observability and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for tenant budget enforcement
function allocate(tenantId, req) {
  const budget = getBudget(tenantId);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) return reject(req, "budget exhausted");
  // consume
  budget.consume(1);
  return forward(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate budget fairness under mixed workloads?
- How would you test the load-shedding policy without impacting real tenants?
